The only structure here is a date paired with an observation. Anything is
permitted: a sentence, a fragment, a figure, a retraction, a note to nobody.
Nothing checks this file and nothing should.

IT IS WRITE-ONLY. Do not read it in the course of ordinary work, in whole or
in part. Git is the archaeology of record: commits and their messages say what
changed, when and why, tied to the diff. Read this only when that has been
tried and has failed.

Write to it at one moment, and it is the compulsion itself rather than any of
its objects: you are about to put into a document something you have just
learned -- why a line reads as it does, what a mistake cost, how a tool
surprised you, that a claim was wrong -- because leaving it unwritten feels
unsafe. That sentence comes here instead. Firing too often is harmless: a
false fire costs one moment's thought and produces the split you wanted
anyway, where a missed one leaves a paragraph of archaeology inside an
instruction for good.

Note what is NOT on that list. Why something was REMOVED is usually a guard --
"why we deleted X" and "don't re-add X" are one sentence -- and guards stay
with the rules, by the don't-do ruling. Only a removal nobody would ever undo,
a rename or a typo or a rephrasing, is archaeology and belongs here.

It is ALSO, deliberately, an escape hatch. If you know a thing is already in
the commit history and cannot leave it unwritten anyway, write it here. That
is the design and not an abuse of it: a redundant paragraph in a file nobody
reads costs nothing, and the same paragraph inside an instruction costs
everyone who executes the instruction.

WHAT IT IS NOT AN ESCAPE HATCH FROM IS THE SPLIT. The rule keeps its guard --
one clause, a date and an outcome, wherever an editor might plausibly undo the
line -- and this file takes the rest. Writing the whole thing here to avoid
deciding which half is the guard is the one use that defeats the purpose:
prohibitions drain into a file nobody reads, and the containment measure
becomes the leak. The test for the guard: would a future editor plausibly undo
the line, and would the sentence stop them?

Do not polish entries. Nothing downstream consumes them.

This is a containment measure, not a fixture. It exists because the models
writing here keep putting history inside instructions; when they stop, it is
retired -- and retiring it is not `rm`: stop routing to it, triage what
accumulated, delete the residue. Written down now because that decision would
otherwise be improvised on a day when the file looks like clutter.

This is not CHANGELOG.md, which faces users and lists what shipped. This faces
whoever works here next, and mostly it faces no one.


